{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1082", "sample_idx": 8, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "The sunlight felt…wrong. Too bright, too perfect. Arthur blinked, trying to clear the fog in his head, but the world remained unnervingly sharp. He sat up in bed, the floral print comforter feeling strangely synthetic against his skin. A digital chime echoed from somewhere nearby, and he glanced at the clock: 6:00 AM. He'd always been a creature of habit, but this morning felt…different. A nagging feeling of deja vu washed over him, a sense of familiarity with every detail of his bedroom – the chipped paint on the windowsill, the dusty photograph of his grandmother on the nightstand. He reached out to touch the photograph, and his fingers passed right through it. Panic flared in his chest. This wasn't his room. This wasn't *real*.\n\nHe stumbled out of bed, his legs feeling strangely disconnected. The hallway was bathed in an artificial glow, the walls smooth and seamless. A pixelated dog barked from outside his door, a sound that felt both comical and deeply unsettling. Looking down, Arthur noticed his clothes – a simple grey t-shirt and jeans – were rendered with uncanny detail, almost like a character in a low-poly RPG. He ran to the mirror, and stared at his reflection. His features were…perfect. Unflawed. He wasn't just in a game; he *was* in a game. A glitch in reality had dropped him into a meticulously crafted digital world, and the only way to escape, he suspected, was to figure out the rules.\n\n\n\n***", "logprobs": [{"token": "The", "logprob": -0.6729406118392944}, {"token": "▁sunlight", "logprob": -0.9427294135093689}, {"token": "▁felt", "logprob": -0.1230592280626297}, {"token": "…", "logprob": -0.02061885967850685}, {"token": "wrong", "logprob": -1.4787518978118896}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -3.659658250398934e-05}, {"token": "▁Too", "logprob": -0.49618053436279297}, {"token": "▁bright", "logprob": -0.02704562619328499}, {"token": ",", "log